{"conjecture": "Long-running software becomes progressively harder to change not because the code degrades, but because each maintainer generation implicitly re-anchors the system's latent invariants to its own internalized model of the domain—models that are never written down and cannot be inferred from the code alone. Because these un-enforced assumptions are epistemically opaque to successors, later maintainers unknowingly violate earlier invariants while making locally correct changes, and the resulting silent drift converts the codebase into an artifact whose behavior depends on a palimpsest of mutually incompatible domain understandings that no single person holds. The system thus becomes rigid not due to accumulated technical complexity, but because the cost of a change becomes dominated by the impossibility of knowing which ghost of a previous maintainer's assumptions the change will silently contradict."}
